.SH NAME
ktm-net.http.middleware \- Request IDs, the real client address, CORS, rate limiting, bearer tokens.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
import net.http.middleware;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Request IDs, the real client address, CORS, rate limiting, bearer tokens.
.SH TYPES
.SS CorsOptions
.EX
struct CorsOptions {
    u8[] origins;
    u8[] methods;
    u8[] headers;
    u8[] expose;
    bool credentials;
    i64 maxAge;
};
.EE
.PP
Which other sites' pages may call the API from a browser
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS requestId
.EX
void requestId(http.Mux *m);
.EE
.PP
Every request gets an ID: the client's X-Request-Id when it sent a sane one (up to 128 letters, digits, - _ . :), else a new UUIDv7; it is in the reply's X-Request-Id and in requestIdOf(r), for logs.
.SS requestIdOf
.EX
u8[] requestIdOf(http.Request *r);
.EE
.PP
The request's ID ("" without the requestId middleware)
.SS realIp
.EX
void realIp(http.Mux *m, u8[] trustedProxies);
.EE
.PP
Behind a proxy or load balancer, r.remoteAddr() is the proxy's. When the request comes from one of trustedProxies ("10.0.0.0/8, 127.0.0.1, ::1": addresses and CIDR ranges), the client is the right-most address of X-Forwarded-For that isn't a trusted proxy, and remoteAddr says so from then on (rate limits and logs see the client). Requests from anywhere else keep their address: X-Forwarded-For can't be faked past it. Add it first. Throws http.ERR_HTTP for a list it can't read.
.SS cors
.EX
void cors(http.Mux *m, CorsOptions *o);
.EE
.PP
Answers browsers' CORS checks (preflight OPTIONS requests included) for the origins in o, and adds the headers to the replies of allowed origins. Add it on the Mux the server serves, not a group: a preflight is answered before routing. o is copied.
.SS rateLimit
.EX
void rateLimit(http.Mux *m, i64 requests, i64 seconds);
.EE
.PP
At most \fBrequests\fR requests every \fBseconds\fR seconds per client address (r.remoteAddr(): add realIp before it behind a proxy), as a token bucket: a quiet client may send \fBrequests\fR at once. Past it: 429 with Retry-After. Shared by every thread of the server; one per Mux or group (a strict one on a login group, a looser one on the whole API).
.SS bearer
.EX
void bearer(http.Mux *m, TokenCheck check);
.EE
.PP
Requests need \fBAuthorization: Bearer <token>\fR that check accepts; else 401 with WWW-Authenticate. check can r.set the user it found, for the handlers (r.get). On a group, it guards the group's routes only.
